{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last ~5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward bias (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR too low across the first ~15–20 forecast days).",
    "Trend/level lag: the forecast path tends to follow the prior level and reacts too slowly to breakouts; apply a faster trend component (increase slope) in the first half of the horizon when momentum is positive.",
    "Peak underestimation: local highs are systematically capped too low (true series makes higher highs than forecast); widen upside range or add extra uplift near projected peaks during strong rallies.",
    "Late-horizon reversal error: beyond ~day 20 the model often stays too high and misses sharp sell-offs (fails to mean-revert downward); introduce stronger mean reversion / downside adjustment in the back half of the horizon, especially after extended rallies.",
    "Oversmoothing around regime shifts: after a sudden drop, the model remains elevated for too long; if recent realized volatility increases or a large down day occurs, pull the entire forecast curve down more aggressively for days ~10–30."
  ]
}
